            TextSection(
                heading = "What Is Reciprocation",
                body = "When someone gives you something — a gift, a favor, information, time, a gesture of goodwill — you experience a psychological pressure to give something back. This is the norm of reciprocity: a deeply embedded social rule that says receiving creates an obligation to return.\n\nSociologist Alvin Gouldner described it in his foundational 1960 paper \"The Norm of Reciprocity: A Preliminary Statement,\" published in the American Sociological Review. He argued that reciprocity is one of the most universal and essential norms in all human societies — not one culture on record lacks it. Without it, Gouldner argued, sustained social cooperation between non-kin would be impossible.\n\nRobert Cialdini identified Reciprocity as one of his six core principles of influence in Influence: The Psychology of Persuasion (1984). His key observation: the obligation is not optional. You don't get to decide whether to feel it. Receiving triggers the norm, and the norm generates a real, uncomfortable sense of being in debt — one that persists until discharged."
            ),
            TextSection(
