                body = "Imagine you're at an event and a friend you like but aren't especially close to pulls out two tickets to a game and gives them to you. Not because you asked. Not because it's your birthday. Just because.\n\nHow do you feel about that friend? Probably warmer than you did five minutes ago. The gift shifts something — they went out of their way for you, they thought of you, they put you in their mind when they didn't have to. That's meaningful.\n\nNow suppose they ask you for a favor later that evening — something that would normally require a bit of effort or inconvenience on your part. Are you more likely to say yes than you would have been before the tickets? Almost certainly. And you can probably feel why: there's a background sense that you owe them something, that saying no would be a kind of social debt left unpaid, that the balance between you is currently tilted and needs evening out.\n\nThat feeling is reciprocity in action. It is not just politeness or fondness. It is a felt obligation, produced automatically by the act of receiving."
