                body = "Dennis Regan at Cornell University tested this in a 1971 study published in the Journal of Experimental Social Psychology, titled \"Effects of a Favor and Liking on Compliance.\"\n\nParticipants arrived for what they believed was an art evaluation task. During a break, a confederate — sometimes returning with a Coca-Cola for the participant, sometimes returning with nothing — later asked participants to buy raffle tickets at 25 cents each. The Coke cost him about 10 cents.\n\nThe result: participants who received the Coke bought roughly twice as many raffle tickets as those who received nothing. The small, uninvited gift had generated a real and measurable increase in compliance with the subsequent request.\n\nThe most important finding in Regan's study was what didn't matter: how much the participants liked the confederate. In conditions where participants had been led to dislike him, receiving the Coke still increased compliance significantly. The norm fired regardless of personal feeling. Reciprocity does not require warmth — it requires only that something was received."
