            TextSection(
                heading = "The Christmas Card Study",
                body = "In 1976, sociologist Phillip Kunz conducted a simple but striking test of the reciprocity norm. He sent Christmas cards — personal, handwritten-style cards — to roughly 600 complete strangers selected from a phone directory. He included his name and address but nothing that would help recipients identify who he was.\n\nThe responses came in: cards, personal letters, family photos, even invitations. About 200 of the 600 strangers wrote back — a response rate of approximately one in three, from people who had no idea who had written to them and no reason to feel any genuine personal connection.\n\nKunz's interpretation was straightforward: receiving a Christmas card from someone — even a stranger — creates sufficient social pressure to send one back. The norm of reciprocity does not require a prior relationship. It requires only that something was given. Once the gift arrives, the obligation forms."
