                body = "Reciprocity is not a cultural preference or a politeness convention that some societies happen to adopt. It is the mechanism that makes sustained cooperation between non-kin possible at all.\n\nIn evolutionary terms: two individuals who each provide and receive help reliably are both better off than two individuals who don't cooperate. But cooperation is vulnerable to defection — one side taking without giving back. Reciprocity is the norm that prevents defection from becoming the dominant strategy. Groups where the norm held together outcompeted groups where it didn't. The brain enforces it with a felt sense of social debt — an uncomfortable, nagging imbalance — because that discomfort is what motivates the return.\n\nThe discomfort of an unpaid social debt is real. People report genuine unease when they receive something they cannot or do not return. This is not irrational sentiment. It is a finely calibrated social monitoring system — tracking the balance of exchange with others and generating motivational pressure to restore it."
            ),
            TextSection(
                heading = "How It's Used in Marketing and Persuasion",
                body = "Reciprocity is one of the most consistently exploited norms in commercial and charitable persuasion, precisely because it cannot be easily switched off — even when recipients know the tactic is being used.\n\nFree samples: The sample at a grocery store or Costco is not just product trial. It is a small gift. The norm activates on receipt, generating a subtle pressure toward purchase that trial alone would not create. Cialdini observed that customers who decline a free sample often feel uncomfortable walking away from the table without buying something.\n\nCharity mailings: Organizations that include address labels, small notepads, or coins in donation requests consistently outperform those that ask without giving first. The gift — even unwanted, even cheap — triggers the norm before the ask arrives.\n\nPharma gifts to doctors: Pharmaceutical representatives offering pens, notepads, lunches, and conference sponsorships to physicians generate measurable effects on prescribing behavior. The relationship is consistent enough that many medical institutions have banned or restricted the practice. The gift does not need to be large. It needs only to be given.\n\nThe door-in-the-face technique: Ask for something very large first. When the other party declines and you scale back to a smaller request, the concession itself is experienced as a gift — you gave something up. Reciprocity fires in response to the concession, increasing compliance with the reduced request.\n\nCialdini's central observation applies to all of these: you cannot opt out of the obligation just because you didn't ask for the gift. The norm is triggered by receiving, not by consent."
            )
