            TextSection(
                heading = "What Is Loss Aversion",
                body = "Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky proposed Prospect Theory in their foundational 1979 paper \"Prospect Theory: An Analysis of Decision under Risk,\" published in Econometrica. One of its central findings: the psychological pain of a loss is approximately twice as powerful as the pleasure of an equivalent gain. Losing \$100 hurts roughly twice as much as gaining \$100 feels good. The relationship is not symmetric, and it is not a matter of personality or mood — it holds consistently across populations, cultures, and domains.\n\nThis asymmetry means that avoiding a loss is a more powerful motivator than acquiring an equivalent gain. People will take greater risks, exert more effort, and tolerate more discomfort to prevent a loss than to achieve something of the same objective value. They do not merely prefer gains — they are disproportionately threatened by losses. The brain does not weigh loss and gain on the same scale. Loss sits on the heavier side, every time."
