                body = "In the original Kahneman and Tversky experiments, participants were presented with choices framed as gains:\n\nOption A: Receive \$3,000 for certain. Option B: An 80% chance of \$4,000, and a 20% chance of nothing.\n\nMost chose Option A — the certain, smaller gain — even though Option B has a higher expected value. The guarantee felt worth more than the probability.\n\nWhen the identical structure was framed as losses:\n\nOption C: Lose \$3,000 for certain. Option D: An 80% chance of losing \$4,000, and a 20% chance of losing nothing.\n\nMost now chose Option D — gambling on the larger potential loss to preserve any chance of losing nothing at all.\n\nSame expected values. Opposite preferences. People are risk-averse when it comes to gains, but risk-seeking when it comes to losses — specifically to avoid the psychological finality of a certain loss. Kahneman won the Nobel Prize in Economics in 2002 partly for this body of work."
            ),
            TextSection(
                heading = "The \"Only 2 Items Left\" Mechanism",
                body = "When a customer encounters a product at a sale price, something shifts psychologically: the sale price becomes a reference point. The customer has anchored to it (Tversky and Kahneman, 1974). This is now what the item \"costs.\" Then comes the constraint: \"Only 2 items left\" or \"Offer valid for 24 hours.\"\n\nAt this moment, the customer is no longer simply deciding whether to buy a product. They are deciding whether to keep or lose the sale price. Not buying means losing access to that reference price — and loss aversion makes this feel significantly more threatening than merely failing to save money would. The emotional framing shifts from \"do I want this?\" to \"will I lose this deal?\"\n\nEvery hour the countdown advances is experienced as moving closer to a confirmed loss. The discomfort is not primarily about the product. It is about the loss of a price the customer has already started to treat as theirs — even though they haven't bought anything yet. The pressure to act comes from loss aversion, not from desire."
            ),
            TextSection(
                heading = "The Endowment Effect — When You've Already Claimed It",
                body = "Related to loss aversion is the endowment effect: once people feel that something belongs to them — even conditionally — they value it significantly more than before ownership. Richard Thaler first described the phenomenon in 1980. Kahneman, Knetsch, and Thaler tested it empirically in a 1990 study published in the Journal of Political Economy.\n\nIn the classic coffee mug experiment, half the participants were given a mug and offered a chance to trade it for a chocolate bar of equivalent market value. Only 11 percent accepted. When the roles were reversed — chocolate first — 89 percent traded. People dramatically overvalued what was already \"theirs,\" even when they had owned it for minutes.\n\nIn a shopping context: a customer who has been looking at a product, added it to a cart, or begun imagining owning it has already started to mentally possess it. When \"only 2 left\" appears, the threat is not just a missed opportunity — it is a loss of something the customer has partially begun to own. Endowment effect plus loss aversion is significantly more powerful than either alone."
