            TextSection(
                heading = "Scarcity, FOMO, and Time Pressure",
                body = "Robert Cialdini, in Influence (1984), identified scarcity as one of the six core principles of persuasion: things become more desirable as they become less available. Scarcity signals demand, raises perceived value, and creates urgency. Loss aversion adds the asymmetric emotional weight on top: missing the scarce item is a felt loss, not a neutral non-event.\n\n\"5 people are looking at this room right now.\" \"3 left in stock.\" \"Price goes up at midnight.\" Each message is engineered to make inaction feel like a loss rather than a simple absence of action. Because losses are registered as roughly twice as powerful as equivalent gains, the urgency created by these messages is disproportionate to the objective stakes involved. The customer feels pressured not because the product is genuinely valuable but because the framing has transformed the decision into one about losing rather than choosing."
            ),
            TextSection(
