                body = "In ancestral environments, the consequences of different types of failure were profoundly asymmetric. Missing a food source was costly — hunger, reduced energy — but survivable. A second attempt was usually possible. Losing shelter, safety, water, or social standing could be permanent and fatal. There was no recovery path.\n\nUnder these conditions, the brain learned to weight potential losses more heavily than equivalent potential gains, because the worst-case outcomes of losses had no second chance. That system is unchanged. The same neural machinery that evolved to prevent genuinely catastrophic and irreversible losses now fires in response to a sale price expiring or a stock counter ticking down. The emotional intensity is entirely out of proportion to the objective stakes. But the mechanism does not update for context — it responds to the signal, not to what the signal is attached to."
