                body = "Loss aversion is among the most deliberately engineered effects in commercial persuasion.\n\nAmazon: \"Only 3 left in stock\" — often displayed even when the scarcity is manufactured rather than genuine. The effect on purchase rate is measurable regardless.\n\nHotel booking sites: \"Last room at this price.\" \"14 people are looking at this hotel right now.\" Both create urgency through scarcity and loss framing simultaneously.\n\nAirlines: Fares that visibly increase as the departure date approaches turn the act of waiting into an active, ongoing loss. Each day of delay is a confirmed cost.\n\nFree trial subscriptions: The trial period creates pseudo-ownership — the endowment effect begins before any payment. When the trial ends, cancellation is experienced not as declining to pay but as losing something already possessed.\n\nRetail: A strikethrough original price (anchor) combined with \"Ends Sunday\" (countdown to loss) creates both a reference point and a deadline. The customer is losing the deal, not gaining a product.\n\nAriely, Loewenstein, and Prelec (2003, Quarterly Journal of Economics — \"Coherent Arbitrariness\") showed that reference points, loss aversion, and anchoring determine willingness-to-pay far more than objective product value does. The customer's psychology constructs the price at least as much as the market does."
